WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.080
Film Courage: Let’s suppose you attend AFM
(American Film Market) this coming year or

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:08.420
next and you’re sitting around with a friendly
first-time filmmaker and you say “You know,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:14.170
I just want to give you 5 tips which you absolutely
must know about making your first feature

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:15.170
film.”

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:24.099
Keith Sutliff: First thing I would say, definitely
have more of a producer’s job still.

00:00:24.099 --> 00:00:28.199
That you have the budgeting and the financing
locked in.

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:33.730
That you have all the money you need for the
entire film from pre-production to principle

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:37.290
photography, post production to distributing
and prints &amp; advertising (P&amp;A).

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.290
Everything.

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:39.290
That it’s locked in.

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:45.570
And then to have a set schedule and time frame
when you want to get this film out there and

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:50.150
not lingering and taking 5 or 10 years but
having a certain timeframe like “Hey, this

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:51.240
is my goal.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:56.410
I want this done, complete, out to the world
by this time frame.”

00:00:56.410 --> 00:01:03.100
There is also preparation with your cinematographer
and other people with the shot list with what

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:07.870
you want, your vision and talking to your
team prior and ‘This is what I think for

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:09.430
these certain scenes.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:10.430
This is how they should look.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:12.260
This is what I want.’

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:19.390
Collaborating with them and also I think it’s
really huge as a director have a good working

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:21.970
relationship with the cast and the crew (but
essentially the cast).

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:28.280
You are working with these people on set and
directing them, what you want them to do and

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:29.280
your vision.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.810
It’s important to where you have a good
friendship and working relationship because

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:36.590
you’re going to spend a lot of time with
these people on set and even (some of them)

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:37.870
in post-production.

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:41.870
You’re still communicating with some of
them depending on the size of the film.

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:46.590
And then when it gets out to everybody, if
you’re doing a theatrical release or premiere,

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.960
everybody is going to come and support the
film.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.010
So it’s good that you have a good working
relationship and you continue to have that.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:56.780
So it’s important I think for any director.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.350
Film Courage: That’s great.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:00.350
Excellent.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.710
Did anyone sit you down and say the same [points
to you]?

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:03.710
Or is this all stuff you learned?

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:04.710
Keith Sutliff: No.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:05.950
Not really.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:06.950
From experience.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:07.950
This is my opinion.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:08.950
Film Courage: Great tips.

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:10.180
Keith Sutliff: Yeah…and definitely the working
relationship.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:14.530
I really want to touch base on that because
if you don’t get along with certain actors

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:18.950
or people as the director, it’s not going
to work.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.650
You’re not going to work.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:21.860
They are not going to respect you.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:27.831
They may not put in all their effort and time
into the scenes and everything you want, so

00:02:27.831 --> 00:02:31.000
you have to have that respect from the cast
and you have to respect them, too.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.020
So it’s a mutual respect.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.360
And the crew, everybody.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.240
It’s a collaboration overall, that’s what
it is (and a business).

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.240
Film Courage: Sure.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.590
And it doesn’t mean that they are bad actors
or bad people.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:44.730
Maybe they didn’t want to really do it [the
film or take on the role] and if you do anything

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.920
with someone who is half-hearted, it creates
issues.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:48.920
Keith Sutliff: Right.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.990
You want to work with people who are passionate
and take direction well.

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:56.930
But also they are suggesting things “What
do you think about this?”

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:59.480
And you open your mind to “Yeah, we can
change that around.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:00.480
That’s cool.”

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:07.420
You open your mind to their suggestions and
collaborating with them and having that continue

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:14.440
on until the film is out in the public and
continuing to work with these people over

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.840
a long time (for a career).

